1. e4 e5 2. d4 exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 4. Qe3 Nf6 5. Nc3 Bb4 6. Bd2 O-O 7. O-O-O Re8
8. Qg3 d6 $6 ({We are still in theory but this may be the game's crisis.} 8...
Nxe4 9. Nxe4 Rxe4 10. c3 Bf8 11. Bd3 {gives White compensation for a pawn}) ({
so} 8... Rxe4 9. Bd3 (9. Nxe4 $2 Nxe4) 9... Rg4 10. Qh3 d6 {is recommended.
Then White might try} 11. f3 Bxc3 12. Bxc3 Ra4 13. Qg3 Rxa2 14. Kb1 {harassing
the rook.}) ({At the post-mortem the two players analysed} 8... Ne5 $5 9. f3 ({
which they improved by} 9. Re1 d6 ({then Hebden tried} 9... Nh5 10. Qh3 g6 11.
Qe3 c6 12. Qd4 {(else d5)} Qa5 ({finally they tried} 12... Bf8 {when} 13. f4
Ng4 14. Nf3 Bg7 {looks OK for Black.}) 13. f4 Bc5 14. Qa4 $1 {when White is
better despite 7 out of 14 moves with his queen.}) 10. f4 Ned7 11. a3 {when
Black is passive.}) 9... c6 10. a3 Ba5) {As the game goes, White preserves his
key e4 pawn then launches a classic attack on Black's vulnerable g7 and f7.} 9.
f3 Ne5 10. h4 c6 11. h5 d5 12. Nge2 Nc4 13. h6 g6 14. Bg5 Qb6 15. Na4 (15. Bxf6
$2 Bd6 $1) 15... Qa5 16. Bxf6 Qxa4 17. Nc3 Bxc3 18. Bxc3 Ne3 19. b3 Qxa2 {
Threatening mate, but Black has too few pieces in action.} 20. Rd2 Qa3+ 21. Bb2
Qe7 22. Be2 $1 {Another sacrifice to open up bishop diagonals.} dxe4 23. fxe4
Qxe4 24. Qg5 Nd5 25. Rxd5 $1 Qxd5 26. Qf6 Kf8 27. Bc4 {An elegant final attack.
Source: The Guardian, 10 September 1994} 1-0

